  "info": {
    "name": "Dalofaelid ",
    "description": "<td valign=\"top\" width=\"450\">\n<h3> Description:</h3>\r\nCoarse, Cromagniform Nordid variety of Central and Northern Europe. Named after the Swedish province of Dalarna and the German region of Westphalia. Typical areas include Central Germany, Netherlands, and Southern Sweden / Norway, partially even Southern Finland. Germanic migrations in Antiquity dispersed it across Europe. A Dalic, Faelid, and V\u00e4stmanland subvariety can be distinguished.\r\n<h3> Physical Traits:</h3>\r\nPale to fair skin. Straight to curly, blonde, sometimes red or brown hair. Grey-blue or mixed eyes. Tall, mesoskelic, mesomorph with robust bones, in old age sometimes endomorph. Mesocephalic, chamae- orthocranic with a curved occiput, very large-headed. Leptorrhine, straight or mildly convex nose. Wide face, broad angular jaw and zygomatic arches. Brow ridges strong, deep-set, small eyes. Forehead broad, mildly sloping. Broad mouth with thin lips. <br/>\n<h3> Literature:</h3>\n<a href=\"http://humanphenotypes.net/links.html#P17\" target=\"_blank\">Paudler (1917)</a>, <a href=\"http://humanphenotypes.net/links.html#K27\" target=\"_blank\">Kern (1927)</a>, <a href=\"http://humanphenotypes.net/links.html#B67\" target=\"_blank\">Biasutti (1967)</a> and Vallois (<a href=\"http://humanphenotypes.net/links.html#V43\" target=\"_blank\">1943</a>, <a href=\"http://humanphenotypes.net/links.html#V71\" target=\"_blank\">1971</a>) called it Dalic, <a href=\"http://humanphenotypes.net/links.html#G42\" target=\"_blank\">G\u00fcnther (1942)</a> Faelid. <a href=\"http://humanphenotypes.net/links.html#V08\" target=\"_blank\">Vondernach (2008)</a> addressed a Dalic and Faelic variety. <a href=\"http://humanphenotypes.net/links.html#E34\" target=\"_blank\">Eickstedt (1934)</a> / <a href=\"http://humanphenotypes.net/links.html#B81\" target=\"_blank\">Baker (1981)</a> use Dalofa(e)lid, Eickstedt (<a href=\"http://humanphenotypes.net/links.html#E37\" target=\"_blank\">1937</a>, <a href=\"http://humanphenotypes.net/links.html#E52\" target=\"_blank\">1952</a>) / <a href=\"http://humanphenotypes.net/links.html#K96\" target=\"_blank\">Knussmann (1996)</a> Dalo Nordid and <a href=\"http://humanphenotypes.net/links.html#L67\" target=\"_blank\">Lundmann (1967)</a> F\u00e4lo Nordid. An eastern branch of <a href=\"http://humanphenotypes.net/links.html#C39\" target=\"_blank\">Coon's (1939)</a> Br\u00fcnn. Confirmed by <a href=\"http://humanphenotypes.net/links.html#S67\" target=\"_blank\">Schwidetzky and Walter (1967)</a>.\r\n </td>"
  },
